[
    {
        "content": "<p>Do we have these in mathlib?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Factorization</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Basic</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">namespace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- The `m`-primary component for a non-zero natural number `n` is the largest divisor of `n`</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">whose prime factors divide `m`. This number has explicit expression `gcd(n, m ^ n)`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">gcd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- The prime-to-`m` component for a non-zero natural number `n` is the largest divisor of `n`</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">which is coprime to `m`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">primeToComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I have developed a few APIs for them, mainly used in <code>m</code>-primary and prime-to-<code>m</code> components of an abelian group. The closest thing I can find in mathlib is <code>p ^ n.factorization p</code> and <code>n / p ^ n.factorization p</code> (only works when <code>p</code> is a prime) with name <code>ordProj</code> and <code>ordCompl</code> in theorem names, and notations <code>ordProj[p] n</code> and <code>ordCompl[p] n</code> (defined in the file <code>Mathlib/Data/Nat/Factorization/Defs.lean</code>; I doubt if any people knows them). These two notation does not render in mathlib docs, though.</p>\n<p>Do you think this generalization should go mathlib?</p>",
        "id": 613132571,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1785221918
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, +1 to this idea. I've been developing a chunk of finite group theory a la Isaacs (Hall subgroups, p-separability / p-length, p-nilpotency) and have been extensively using unitary divisors to describe the orders of Hall subgroups and their complements:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat.IsUnitaryDivisor</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">dvd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∣</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coprime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat.Coprime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Subgroup.IsHall</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Subgroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coprime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat.Coprime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat.card</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H.index</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>so that <code>H.IsHall ↔ (Nat.card H).IsUnitaryDivisor (Nat.card G)</code>. As unitary divisors are essentially the predicate version of <code>primaryComponent</code>, they complement eachother nicely.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">d.IsUnitaryDivisor</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n.primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>(even the <code>n = 0</code> / <code>d = 0</code> junk values agree).</p>\n<p>Looking at my code, \"primaryComponent is the nicer spelling when you're actually building the component (most cleanly on the abelian side). <code>IsUnitaryDivisor</code> seems preferrable for things like Hall subgroups where the order is given.</p>\n<p>In terms of API, I lean on the factorization characterization: for <code>d ≠ 0</code>, <code>n ≠ 0</code>,</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">d.IsUnitaryDivisor</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d.factorization</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d.factorization</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n.factorization</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>so I'd suggest a similar API for <code>primaryComponent</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n.primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">factorization</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n.factorization.filter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∣</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and an <code>_apply</code> lemma</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n.primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">factorization</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∣</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n.factorization</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>makes everything downstream simple arithmetic (avoiding <code>n.gcd (m ^ n)</code>).</p>\n<p>I'm planning to push my code to my fork in the next week or so. Happy to help get this stuff into mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 613317526,
        "sender_full_name": "Richard Osborn",
        "timestamp": 1785286480
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for the reply. My version had just pushed to my repository <a href=\"https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/master/Iwasawalib/Data/Nat/PrimaryComponent.lean\">https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/master/Iwasawalib/Data/Nat/PrimaryComponent.lean</a>. Feel free to take some of them for you project and/or PR for mathlib. The application for <code>m</code>-primary and prime-to-<code>m</code> components of an abelian group is in <a href=\"https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/master/Iwasawalib/GroupTheory/Torsion.lean\">https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/master/Iwasawalib/GroupTheory/Torsion.lean</a>.</p>\n<p>Your <code>Nat.IsUnitaryDivisor</code> is good, which is also used in Atkin-Lehner operator for modular forms I think.</p>\n<p>Why I use <code>n.gcd (m ^ n)</code> is that I think it's more elementary than <code>∏ p ∈ m.primeFactors, p ^ n.factorization p</code>. One can easily prove that <code>n.gcd (m ^ n)</code> indeed satisfy the property mentioned in docstring:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dvd_primaryComponent_iff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∣</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primaryComponent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∣</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primeFactors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primeFactors</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I think your <code>(n.primaryComponent m).factorization = n.factorization.filter (· ∣ m)</code> is interesting. Currently I only have <code>(n.primaryComponent m).factorization p = if p ∈ m.primeFactors then n.factorization p else 0</code>.</p>",
        "id": 613356306,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1785309227
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>A few (maybe not so important) API is missing, e.g. <code>n.primaryComponent m</code> depends only in <code>m.primeFactors</code>.</p>",
        "id": 613356635,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1785309340
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>A search shows that notations <code>ordProj[</code> and <code>ordCompl[</code> only appeared in 3 mathlib files:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Data/Nat/Factorization/Defs.lean\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Data/Nat/Factorization/Defs.lean</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Data/Nat/Factorization/Basic.lean\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Data/Nat/Factorization/Basic.lean</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Data/Nat/Factorization/PrimePow.lean\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Data/Nat/Factorization/PrimePow.lean</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Maybe we should migrate them to <code>primaryComponent</code> and <code>primeToComponent</code>? What are the opinions in community?</p>",
        "id": 613357324,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1785309611
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(... or maybe keep them in their current forms, since they look like <code>MonoidHom</code>?)</p>",
        "id": 613357644,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1785309737
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I like <code>n.gcd (m ^ n)</code> as a definition. Just don't think you'd want to work with it directly.</p>",
        "id": 613413226,
        "sender_full_name": "Richard Osborn",
        "timestamp": 1785327935
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In another branch <a href=\"https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/ordproj/Iwasawalib/Data/Nat/PrimaryComponent.lean\">https://github.com/acmepjz/lean-iwasawa/blob/ordproj/Iwasawalib/Data/Nat/PrimaryComponent.lean</a> I tried <code>MonoidHom</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"sd\">/-- `Nat.ordProj m` is the `m`-primary component map `ℕ →*₀ ℕ`, which maps `0` to `0`, and maps a</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">non-zero `n` to the largest divisor of `n` whose prime factors divide `m`, which has explicit</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">expression `gcd(n, m ^ n)`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ordProj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→*₀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">toFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">gcd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 613608898,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1785405079
    }
]